# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/010_integer_division_modulo.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 010_integer_division_modulo
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Integer division truncating toward zero and a dividend-signed remainder, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Integer division has exactly two undefined cases and both are provably absent from this
program.  No divisor is ever zero: in the folded variant every divisor is a nonzero literal,
and in the runtime variant every divisor is a plain object staged from a volatile object
initialised to 2, -2 or 3 -- r_b_pos from b_pos, r_b_neg from b_neg, r_three from three,
r_u_two from u_two and r_l_two from l_two -- and each initialiser is visible in the source, so
no divisor can be zero however the reads are ordered.  The most negative value divided by -1,
which overflows and is therefore undefined, is deliberately never formed: the large-magnitude
case uses -2147483647, which is one greater than the most negative int, and divides it by 3
rather than by -1.
No signed overflow occurs anywhere else either.  Every quotient is representable in its own
type, and the widest product any identity check forms is 6 in magnitude, so no
multiplication can overflow.  No shift operator appears anywhere in the program.  There is
no aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the
relative addresses of unrelated objects.
NOTHING DEPENDS ON UNSPECIFIED EVALUATION ORDER, and that is enforced structurally rather
than argued.  An access to a volatile object is an observable side effect, and C11 6.5.2.2p10
leaves the order of evaluation of a call's function designator, of its arguments, and of
subexpressions within those arguments unspecified.  The runtime variant therefore STAGES every
volatile datum before any call: each of the ten volatile objects is read exactly once, in a
full expression of its own, into a plain object -- r_a_pos, r_a_neg, r_b_pos, r_b_neg,
r_big_neg, r_three, r_u_seven, r_u_two, r_l_neg7 and r_l_two -- and every quotient, every
remainder and both identity results are then computed from those plain objects in statements of
their own.  The calls pass plain objects only, so no argument list contains a side effect at
all and the suite's authoring rule of at most one side-effecting argument per call is satisfied
by construction.  Each volatile object is additionally written exactly once, by its own
initialiser in its own declaration, so no object is modified twice between sequence points.
Staging costs the test nothing: a value that arrived through a volatile load stays opaque to
the optimizer, so every division and every remainder is still emitted as a run-time operation.
That was verified at instruction level at -O2 on all four targets, and the counts appear in
impl_defined_notes below.
Truncation toward zero and a remainder taking the sign of the dividend are not
implementation-defined choices this program rests on: C11 6.5.5p6 makes / yield the
algebraic quotient with any fractional part discarded and requires (a / b) * b + a % b to
equal a wherever the quotient is representable, from which the remainder's sign follows.
The two identity lines assert that law directly, and both operands of every comparison in
them share a signedness, so no implicit signed-to-unsigned comparison is formed and the
full default warning gate passes with no deviation at all.
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer checks division by zero and the most negative value divided by
-1 specifically, and reports nothing for this program. That is measured evidence rather than an
assertion that both undefined cases are absent -- and it is exactly as much evidence as the
suite's sanitizer gate produces: ONE instrumented native build, dynamically linked and driven by
the reference compiler, and then executed once. The gate does not sweep the optimization levels
or the cross targets, so no claim is made here about behaviour under -O1 or -O2 that the run did
not establish; the argument above, which is about the operand values rather than the code
generated for them, is what covers every cell.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This program takes NO target restriction, and the reason is NORMATIVE rather than empirical.
C11 6.5.5p6 makes / yield the algebraic quotient with any fractional part discarded -- which is
truncation toward zero -- and requires (a / b) * b + a % b to equal a wherever the quotient is
representable, from which the remainder's sign follows: it must take the sign of the dividend.
Neither is an implementation-defined choice a target is free to make differently, so every
value this program prints is fixed by the standard on any conforming implementation and the
comparison is portable across all four backends by construction.  (That was not always so:
C89 left the direction of truncation for negative operands implementation-defined, and C99
closed it.  This corpus is compiled in a C11-or-later mode on both sides, so the C11 rule is
the one in force.)  Measurement CORROBORATES that and is retained for exactly that role, never
as the basis: -7 / 2 yields -3 and -7 % 2 yields -1 on x86-64, i686, AArch64 and RISC-V 64
alike, and all twelve target-and-optimization configurations printed the same 319 bytes.  So a
cross-backend divergence in this program is a genuine finding to report rather than an
implementation-defined difference to mark, and oracle (b) is enabled for it.
The lowering differs sharply per target even though the results agree, which is what makes
the runtime variant valuable.  Measured at -O2 with gcc 13.4.0 on the staged source as
committed: x86-64 and i686 each emit six idivl, a single instruction that yields the quotient
and the remainder at once, plus one divl for the unsigned pair; AArch64 has no remainder
instruction, so its seven sdiv and one udiv are each paired with an msub that synthesises the
remainder -- eight msub for the program's eight remainders; and RISC-V 64 uses separate
instructions throughout, six divw with six remw, one divuw with one remuw, and one div with
one rem.  The 64-bit signed case is where the two x86 targets part company: x86-64 divides in
registers with cqto and idivq, while i686 has no 64-bit divide instruction at all and calls
the libgcc helper __divmoddi4, which returns the quotient and the remainder together -- static
linkage is what resolves that helper with no dynamic loader involved.  Those counts are also
the instruction-level evidence that staging folded nothing away.
long and unsigned long are deliberately never used.  Their width was measured at 4 bytes on
i686 and 8 bytes on the other three targets, so dividing a long would print two different
values across backends for reasons that have nothing to do with the compiler.  Only int and
unsigned int, 32 bits on all four targets, and long long, 64 bits on all four, appear here.
No object of plain char type is declared and no plain-char value is printed, because plain
char signedness was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V
64.  The only char in the program is the const char * parameter of the hand-declared printf
prototype, where no signedness is observable.  No pointer value is printed and no header is
included, so every bound this program relies on is spelled literally in its own source.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_pos_pos=3 1
fold_neg_pos=-3 -1
fold_pos_neg=-3 1
fold_neg_neg=3 -1
fold_exact=-4 0
fold_large=-715827882 -1
fold_unsigned=3 1
fold_llong=-3 -1
fold_identity=1
run_pos_pos=3 1
run_neg_pos=-3 -1
run_pos_neg=-3 1
run_neg_neg=3 -1
run_exact=-4 0
run_large=-715827882 -1
run_unsigned=3 1
run_llong=-3 -1
run_identity=1
END
